# Architecture Plan

## Product Scope

Build the repository as a Next.js App Router application for daily tracking of papers and GitHub repositories. The product name should continue to come from GitHub issues and any future `PRODUCT.md`; do not introduce agent names or placeholders.

## Module Structure

- `src/app`: App Router routes, layouts, route handlers, loading/error boundaries, and server actions where appropriate.
- `src/components`: Reusable UI components, split by domain (`paper`, `repository`, `dashboard`, `filters`, `export`, `shell`) and low-level primitives.
- `src/lib`: Shared runtime utilities, environment parsing, date grouping, language helpers, fetch clients, export helpers, and error handling.
- `src/services`: Data-access and business workflows for papers, repositories, summaries, notes, tags, ingest, search, and scheduled fetches.
- `src/validators`: Zod schemas for route inputs, import payloads, fetcher outputs, and service boundaries.
- `src/types`: Shared TypeScript types inferred from Zod and Prisma where useful; avoid duplicate hand-written model shapes.
- `src/llm`: LLM client abstraction, prompt templates, bilingual summary parsing, retries, and provider-specific adapters.
- `src/fetchers`: arXiv, GitHub, generic web, and PDF metadata fetchers with normalized result contracts.
- `src/export`: Markdown, JSON, and CSV export formatters.
- `prisma`: Prisma schema, migrations, seed script, and generated client integration.
- `tests`: Unit tests for fetchers, summarization, exports, services, plus end-to-end workflow coverage.

## Shared Data Model

- Model content as a common item concept with separate `Paper` and `Repository` tables for source-specific fields.
- Store bilingual summaries in a related `Summary` model keyed by item type, item id, language, summary style, and generated metadata.
- Store `Tag` and `Note` as reusable bilingual-aware entities linked to either papers or repositories.
- Track ingestion provenance with source URL, canonical URL, fetch status, dedupe keys, timestamps, archive/important flags, and error fields.
- Keep Prisma as the database source of truth and derive API/service types from Prisma plus Zod validation at boundaries.

## Issue Dependencies

- Issues 1-4 establish the application shell, Tailwind, linting, TypeScript, dark mode, responsiveness, and language toggle scaffolding.
- Issues 3-8 establish persistence: PostgreSQL, Prisma, data models, validation, service layer, and seed data.
- Issues 9-13 implement ingestion sources and endpoints: arXiv, GitHub repositories, generic web/PDF metadata, batch URL import, and Markdown import.
- Issues 14-17 add summarization: LLM abstraction, paper/repo summary endpoints, and automatic summarization on ingest.
- Issues 18-26 build the primary user workflow: dashboard, cards, details, add-item UI, search/filter/sort, notes/tags, and item actions.
- Issues 27-29 implement export formats and UI controls.
- Issues 30-31 add scheduled or manual discovery jobs for arXiv keywords and GitHub trending.
- Issues 32-34 harden the product with unit tests, E2E coverage, error states, loading/empty states, and responsive review.
- Issue 35 finalizes README and run documentation after implementation details stabilize.

## Key Design Decisions

- Use server-first Next.js App Router patterns for data loading and mutations; only use client components for interactive UI state.
- Centralize all database writes through services so ingestion, summarization, UI edits, imports, and scheduled jobs share dedupe and validation behavior.
- Normalize fetcher outputs before persistence so source-specific parsing does not leak into UI components or export code.
- Keep bilingual support explicit with `en` and `zh` language fields or rows, not ad hoc string suffixes in UI logic.
- Make summarization provider-swappable behind a small interface so tests can use deterministic fakes.
- Prefer idempotent ingestion endpoints with canonical URL and source-specific IDs to avoid duplicate papers or repositories.
- Build exports from service-layer DTOs rather than UI components to keep formats stable and testable.
- Add focused tests alongside each feature with broader E2E coverage once the ingest-to-summary-to-export workflow exists.
